THE REAL SUBJECT:

docs: a ```ignore fence now has to SAY WHY — and 48 of the 71 turned out to just be broken

A doctest is the one form of documentation the compiler can check. This tree had 71 fences
marked ```ignore, and not one said why — structurally none COULD, because a fence
info-string rejects unknown attributes: there is nowhere to put a reason. So the question
"is this off because it cannot compile here, or because it stopped compiling and someone
silenced it?" had no answer anywhere in the tree.

It has one now, and it is mostly the second. Positionally matched against the base tree, the
fate of all 71:

    35  now compile AND RUN, with no marker at all
    13  ```no_run — compile and LINK, but must not execute
    15  ```ignore WITH a stated structural reason
     1  ```text — never was Rust
     7  untouched remainder, baselined (4 vendored upstream, 3 owned by a concurrent lane)

This commit carries the shared instrument plus my own 15 fences; the app-framework half
(29 repairs) landed in a73e2ba72 and the leaf-crate half (16) in 24c004a4f.

THE INSTRUMENT (`scripts/check-bare-ignore.py --fences`)

  It lives beside the `#[ignore]` attribute gate because it is the same disease one altitude
  up: a test switched off, and a documentation example switched off. Two contracts:

    F1  a ```ignore fence whose first CONTENT line is not `// IGNORED: <reason>`. A
        `#`-hidden reason does not count — rustdoc hides it, and the reader is who the
        reason is for.
    F2  an INERT ATTRIBUTE: an info-string mixing a doctest attribute with a token rustdoc
        does not know (```ascii,no_run). rustdoc reads any unknown token as "not Rust", so
        the attribute beside it silently does nothing. That is the gating-defaults-to-silence
        class, in a code fence. Our own code has zero; the eight in the tree are upstream
        curve25519-dalek's AVX2 lane diagrams.

  It is NOT a grep — and, the part worth knowing, it is not `strip_noise` either. That
  function BLANKS comments, and a doctest fence lives inside one, so reusing it would have
  reported ZERO on a tree with 71 findings. This is the same lexer run inside-out: it keeps
  `///` / `//!` / `/** */` and skips string literals, char literals, plain `//` comments and
  `////` banners. Three shapes in this tree need exactly that, and each is a `-red` case: a
  ```ignore line INSIDE an already-open ```text block (content, not an opener), the marker
  named in prose, and a fence body inside a raw string literal — this repo's own gate scripts
  embed fence text constantly.

  THE FLOOR: it asserts it SAW at least 500 fences (676 today). A negative headline passes
  just as happily when the reader is broken and sees nothing at all.

  THE BASELINE (`scripts/doctest-ignore-baseline.tsv`) RATCHETS. A row is keyed on (path,
  hash of the fence BODY), so it survives the file moving around the fence and DIES when the
  example is rewritten. A row that matches nothing FAILS the gate: a repaired fence retires
  its own exemption, or the file decays into a list of things that used to be wrong. There is
  deliberately no regenerate flag — adding a row is supposed to cost a sentence.

  RED-PROOF, three ways. `--fences --self-test` drives 7 disease shapes and 12 shapes a naive
  grep gets wrong here, plus 3 ratchet properties (exempts the right one; FAILS on a stale
  row; an empty baseline leaves every finding live). Withholding the baseline on the live
  tree reports all 15 baselined findings. And before the repairs the same sweep printed 13 F1
  rows across app-framework, sdk and the askama vendor drop.

